'''''Festuca donax''''' is a species of [[Poaceae|grass]]. It is native to the [[Madeira]] archipelago in the Atlantic Ocean.<ref name = powo/>

==Description==
The plant is [[perennial plant|perennial]] and caespitose with {{convert|60|–|120|cm}} long [[culm (botany)|culms]] that grow in a [[Sessility (zoology)|clump]]. The [[ligule]] is {{convert|3|–|5|mm}} long and is going around the eciliate membrane. Leaf-blades are {{convert|5|–|10|cm}} broad with scabrous margins. The [[panicle]] is elliptic, open, [[inflorescence]]d and is {{convert|18|–|30|cm}} long. [[Spikelet]]s are elliptic, solitary, {{convert|5.5|–|6.5|mm}} long, and carry fertile ones which have 2–3 fertile [[floret]]s that are  diminished at the apex.<ref name=kew>{{cite web|url= https://www.kew.org/data/grasses-db/www/imp04533.htm|author1=W.D. Clayton |author2=M. Vorontsova |author3=K.T. Harman |author4=H. Williamson |title=Festuca donax|publisher= GrassBase|work=The Board of Trustees and [[Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew|Royal Botanic Gardens]]|place=[[Kew]]|date=November 16, 2012|accessdate=December 6, 2013}}</ref>

The [[chaff|glumes]] are chartaceous, lanceolate, keelless, with acuminate apexes, with only difference is in size. The upper one is {{convert|5|–|6.2|mm}} long while the other one is {{convert|4.5|–|5.3|mm}} long. Fertile [[lemma (botany)|lemma]] is {{convert|4.4|–|5.2|mm}} long and is also chartaceous, ovate and keelless. Lemma itself is muticous with acute apex and scaberulous surface. [[Flower]]s have a hairy [[ovary (botany)|ovary]] and three [[stamen]]s that are {{convert|3|mm}} long. The [[fruit]]s are [[caryopsis|caryopses]] with an additional [[pericarp]], which just like flowers is hairy as well. [[Hilum (biology)|Hilum]] is linear.<ref name=kew/>

